1
00:00:00,364 --> 00:00:02,346
It was supposed to be a normal night.

2
00:00:02,346 --> 00:00:06,502
Just Milo, his phone, and absolutely no plans to improve his life.

3
00:00:07,173 --> 00:00:08,755
He was scrolling through videos,

4
00:00:08,755 --> 00:00:12,587
the ancient human ritual of doing nothing while feeling extremely busy.

5
00:00:14,355 --> 00:00:18,155
Then his phone displayed something unusual. Not a message. Not an update.

6
00:00:18,155 --> 00:00:20,112
Something that looked… very wrong.

7
00:00:20,544 --> 00:00:23,144
A strange signal slipped through his earphones.

8
00:00:23,144 --> 00:00:26,126
And somehow, it felt like the room itself was listening.

9
00:00:26,705 --> 00:00:29,198
Milo tried to pull them out. A little too late.

10
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:31,620
And then… nothing.

11
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:35,642
The kind of nothing that usually means tomorrow is going to be complicated.

12
00:00:39,049 --> 00:00:41,358
The next morning, Milo woke up with a headache,

13
00:00:41,358 --> 00:00:44,703
a dead phone, and no memory of the weirdest part of his night.

14
00:00:45,135 --> 00:00:48,008
Then he heard a voice. Not from the street. Not from the phone.

15
00:00:48,008 --> 00:00:50,019
From inside someone else’s head.

16
00:00:50,570 --> 00:00:52,968
Please don’t die again. I just bought those plants.

17
00:00:53,496 --> 00:00:55,551
He could hear thoughts. Actual thoughts.

18
00:00:55,551 --> 00:01:00,052
Which meant one thing: his brain had either upgraded… or completely given up.

19
00:01:00,634 --> 00:01:02,325
The neighbor waved politely.

20
00:01:02,325 --> 00:01:07,492
Meanwhile, her mind was busy remembering groceries and negotiating with a dying fern.

21
00:01:08,074 --> 00:01:08,654
Naturally,

22
00:01:08,674 --> 00:01:13,292
Milo did what any reasonable person would do after gaining a supernatural ability.

23
00:01:13,292 --> 00:01:14,942
He went outside to test it.

24
00:01:15,009 --> 00:01:19,082
One person was thinking about bread. Another was thinking about a missing key.

25
00:01:19,082 --> 00:01:24,226
A third was apparently thinking about lunch. Humanity was not mysterious. It was hungry.

26
00:01:24,613 --> 00:01:29,754
Then someone smiled at him. Milo smiled back. A perfectly normal social interaction.

27
00:01:30,288 --> 00:01:33,090
Wait… why is he smiling? Do I have something on my face?

28
00:01:33,691 --> 00:01:38,473
Milo suddenly became very aware of his own existence. Every step felt awkward.

29
00:01:38,473 --> 00:01:40,253
Every movement felt visible.

30
00:01:40,833 --> 00:01:44,615
But most people were too busy thinking about themselves to study him.

31
00:01:44,615 --> 00:01:49,254
That’s the spotlight effect: we often imagine we’re the center of everyone’s attention.

32
00:01:49,837 --> 00:01:52,292
And then he discovered something even stranger.

33
00:01:52,292 --> 00:01:56,150
People could say one thing… while thinking something completely different.

34
00:01:56,725 --> 00:02:01,070
At a café, the cashier said, ’Have a wonderful day.’ Her thoughts were mostly about the

35
00:02:01,070 --> 00:02:04,612
long line and the coffee machine making a suspicious noise.

36
00:02:05,037 --> 00:02:07,419
People manage the impressions they give others.

37
00:02:07,419 --> 00:02:10,110
They try to be polite, friendly, and appropriate.

38
00:02:10,110 --> 00:02:13,086
Their private thoughts, however, are not always polished.

39
00:02:13,713 --> 00:02:17,910
At first, reading minds felt like having the world’s most exclusive information.

40
00:02:18,342 --> 00:02:21,942
But the more people he passed, the more thoughts arrived. One after another.

41
00:02:21,942 --> 00:02:23,264
Then all at once.

42
00:02:24,178 --> 00:02:26,905
Did I lock the door? What’s for dinner? I should call Mom.

43
00:02:26,905 --> 00:02:29,100
Why is that song still playing in my head?

44
00:02:30,485 --> 00:02:33,940
His brain had become a browser with forty-seven tabs open…

45
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:36,031
and every tab was playing audio.

46
00:02:36,557 --> 00:02:39,702
The human brain filters information constantly.

47
00:02:39,702 --> 00:02:43,375
Attention helps us focus on what matters and ignore the rest.

48
00:02:43,375 --> 00:02:46,170
Milo’s new power seemed to have removed the filter.

49
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:48,186
He wasn’t just hearing people.

50
00:02:48,186 --> 00:02:51,334
He was trying to process too much information at the same time.

51
00:02:51,764 --> 00:02:54,912
For the first time, Milo wished he could go back to not knowing.

52
00:02:55,540 --> 00:02:58,651
Then he spotted a cat. Surely, a cat’s mind would be simpler.

53
00:02:59,080 --> 00:03:02,736
He focused. He listened. He waited for the secrets of the universe.

54
00:03:03,260 --> 00:03:06,078
The cat’s entire universe appeared to involve food,

55
00:03:06,078 --> 00:03:09,061
comfort, and a door that someone had failed to open.

56
00:03:09,490 --> 00:03:12,945
Milo had crossed dimensions, survived a mysterious signal,

57
00:03:12,945 --> 00:03:16,176
and been defeated by an animal that didn’t even acknowledge him.

58
00:03:17,327 --> 00:03:20,629
As the day slowed down, Milo began to understand something.

59
00:03:21,057 --> 00:03:24,966
A thought is not always a belief. A passing worry is not a plan.

60
00:03:24,966 --> 00:03:28,472
And a strange thought doesn’t automatically reveal who someone is.

61
00:03:28,899 --> 00:03:30,372
People’s minds are messy.

62
00:03:30,372 --> 00:03:34,808
They contain memories, guesses, fears, jokes, and random nonsense.

63
00:03:34,808 --> 00:03:38,194
Understanding someone takes more than collecting their private thoughts.

64
00:03:38,623 --> 00:03:41,005
Real understanding comes from paying attention,

65
00:03:41,005 --> 00:03:43,957
asking, listening, and sometimes simply helping.

66
00:03:44,384 --> 00:03:48,529
Maybe knowing everything about someone isn’t the same as understanding them.

67
00:03:48,529 --> 00:03:50,821
Maybe respect matters more than access.

68
00:03:51,425 --> 00:03:55,385
Milo walked home, hoping the whole mind-reading thing would finally stop.

69
00:03:55,815 --> 00:04:00,661
He put his phone away. No scrolling. No earphones. No mysterious signals. Just peace.

70
00:04:01,236 --> 00:04:03,984
Then, from somewhere outside, he heard one tiny thought.

71
00:04:04,515 --> 00:04:06,109
Feed me. And… open the door.

72
00:04:06,584 --> 00:04:09,934
Milo sighed. Tomorrow was going to be a very long day.

73
00:04:11,061 --> 00:04:13,865
Because apparently, even silence had a lot to say.

